Chapter: Fat16Dirgrow
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16

 BACKLOG 7.15: a subdirectory that has run out of slots grows a cluster
 instead of refusing a write the volume has room for.

 The fixture's clusters are one sector, so SUB holds sixteen 32-byte slots and
 two of them are . and .. -- fourteen files fit in the cluster it was born
 with. The fifteenth is the whole test. Before the growth code it answered
 False on a disk with sixteen megabytes free, and that False was
 indistinguishable from the honest one a full ROOT gives.

 Fourteen-then-one is deliberate. A test that wrote fifteen files and checked
 only the count would pass on a volume that grew on the first write and on one
 that never needed to, so it would not be evidence about growth at all. The
 fill is sized to leave the directory exactly full, which makes the next write
 the first one that cannot be served without a new cluster.

 Three assertions, and the count is the weakest of them:

 The chain length is the direct evidence. SUB is resolved and its cluster
 chain is walked through the FAT: one cluster before the fifteenth file, two
 after. A write that reported True while quietly landing the entry somewhere
 else would not move this number.

 The read-back of the FIRST file is what catches a growth that corrupts what
 was already there. The new cluster is zeroed before it is linked, and the
 link is written last; if either were done in the other order, an existing
 entry or a stale one would show up here.

 F15 must NOT be in the root. A writer that gave up on the parent and fell
 back to the root would report True and read back through SUB/ correctly if
 the walk found it there -- reading the root is what tells those apart, the
 same way fat16-subdir pins it.

 We say:

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Device.Block] Nothing
  opening = act
    filled <- fill-dir 1 14 0
    print-line-uni ("filled " & show filled)

    vol <- fat16-init 2048
    before <- sub-chain-length vol
    print-line-uni ("clusters-before " & show before)

    grew <- fat16-write-file "SUB/F15.TXT" "past the first cluster"
    print-line-uni ("fifteenth " & show grew)

    vol2 <- fat16-init 2048
    after <- sub-chain-length vol2
    print-line-uni ("clusters-after " & show after)

    back <- fat16-read-text vol2 "SUB/F15.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("readback " & maybe-text back)

    first <- fat16-read-text vol2 "SUB/F1.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("first-intact " & maybe-text first)

    root <- fat16-read-text vol2 "F15.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("not-in-root " & maybe-text root)
  end

Section: Filling

 Each write allocates a cluster for the file's own bytes as well as taking a
 slot, so this exercises the allocator fourteen times over before the
 directory itself needs anything.

  fill-dir : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Block] Integer
  fill-dir (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i > n then acc
    else act
      ok <- fat16-write-file ("SUB/F" & show i & ".TXT") ("body " & show i)
      fill-dir (i + 1) n (acc + (if ok then 1 else 0))
    end

Section: Measuring The Chain

 The walk stops on anything a chain cannot continue into, not only on the
 end-of-chain marker, so a corrupt volume ends this loop rather than hanging
 the machine.

  sub-chain-length : Fat16Volume -> [Device.Block] Integer
  sub-chain-length (vol) = act
    e <- fat16-resolve-path vol "SUB"
    chain-from vol (entry-cluster e) 0
  end

  chain-from : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Block] Integer
  chain-from (vol) (c) (acc) =
    if c < 2 then acc
    else if fat16-is-end c then acc
    else act
      next <- fat16-next-cluster vol c
      chain-from vol next (acc + 1)
    end

Section: Reporting

  maybe-text : Maybe Text -> Text
  maybe-text (m) = when m
    is Just (t) -> t
    is None -> "<none>"

  entry-cluster : Maybe Fat16DirEntry -> Integer
  entry-cluster (m) = when m
    is Just (e) -> e.de-cluster
    is None -> 0
